    "result_text": "Done. The full build is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the disposable-domain email blocklist as an OfficeFloor validation step, following the project's existing endpoint patterns (mirroring the postcode/registration-date validators).\n\n**New files:**\n- `OwnerEmails.java` (extended) \u2014 added the blocklist `Set` (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`) plus `domainOf(...)` and `isDisposableDomain(...)`, keeping email-domain logic in the existing single-source-of-truth helper.\n- `ValidateOwnerEmailDomain.java` \u2014 a function step that reads the validated body (`@Val OwnerFieldsDto`) and, **only when an email is present**, throws the checked `DisposableEmailDomainException` for a blocklisted domain.\n- `DisposableEmailDomainException.java` \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`).\n- `DisposableEmailDomainExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `400` via `ProblemDetail`, matching the other escalation handlers.\n- `...DisposableEmailDomainException.yml` \u2014 escalation wiring.\n\n**Wiring:** inserted `validateEmailDomain` after `validatePostcode` in both `owners.POST.yml` and `{ownerId}.PUT.yml` (both pipelines set the owner's email), under `govern: [ transaction ]`.\n\nAn absent email is still accepted (consistent with the postcode/registration-date \"only when present\" rules), and the check runs after body validation so a malformed body still yields its own 400.",
