    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a faithful, additive clone of the codebase's established \"validate an optional owner field\" family, and every unit lands where a maintainer would expect:\n\n- **`ValidateOwnerEmailDomain`** mirrors `ValidateOwnerPostcode` exactly: same `service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto)` signature, same present-only guard (`if (email == null) return;`), same delegation to a domain helper, same checked-exception throw, same Javadoc shape. A maintainer who knows the postcode step will read this instantly.\n- **`DisposableEmailDomainException` + `DisposableEmailDomainExceptionHandler`** are carbon copies of the `InvalidPostcodeException`/`InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler` pair, including the `ProblemDetails.build(...)` \u2192 `ResponseEntity.status(...).body(detail)` convention shared by all handlers. No divergence.\n- **Logic placement in `OwnerEmails`** is coherent, not catch-all. The class is already the single source of truth for owner-email values (used by `BuildOwner`/`ApplyOwner` for `normalize`); `domainOf` is a natural normalization-adjacent accessor, and owning the blocklist policy parallels how `OwnerPostcodes` owns `isValidForCity`. The class doc was updated to reflect the added responsibility.\n- **YAML wiring** is consistent: the step is inserted at the same point in both POST and PUT pipelines (after `validatePostcode`, before the load/normalize work), carries `govern: [ transaction ]` like its neighbors, and the escalation file follows the `<fqcn>.yml` \u2192 handler convention.\n\nNothing is duplicated at the structural level and no class or method is becoming a grab-bag. (The only micro-observation \u2014 `domainOf` being recomputed once inside `isDisposableDomain` and again to build the exception message \u2014 is a correctness/efficiency detail on the rejection path, explicitly out of scope here, and reads cleanly either way.)",
